WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:05.140
The Boys openly calls out superhero cliches
and gets really meta about comic books.

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:06.060
And we love it!

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:11.330
From MCU potshots to Justice League shout-outs,
here are small details that only true fans

00:00:11.330 --> 00:00:13.000
noticed about Amazon's The Boys.

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:17.540
It doesn't take long to get familiar with
the superheroes or "supes" in the world of

00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:19.360
The Boys, because we already know them.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:22.910
The production is counting on the fact that
its audience is already familiar with the

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:26.670
slew of superheroes that have quickly become
mainstream over the past few years.

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:29.990
Thus, many of the supes need no introduction
or explanation.

00:00:29.990 --> 00:00:34.370
We know that Homelander is a Superman clone,
The Deep is this world's Aquaman, and Queen

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:35.680
Maeve is Wonder Woman.

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:38.600
Vought's"The Seven" are clearly DC's Justice
League.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:40.160
So where are the Avengers?

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:44.920
They exist, we learn, through a quick quip
from Starlight you may have overlooked.

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:48.999
In episode one, Starlight worries that the
"Crimson Countess" got the open position in

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:50.719
the Seven rather than her.

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:52.880
"Besides, Countess probably got it.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:53.700
She's so good."

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:58.800
The Crimson Countess is a member of the Avengers-esque
superhero team called Payback, and she's an

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:03.380
obvious riff on Scarlet Witch, the Vision-loving
Avenger played by Elizabeth Olsen.

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:08.540
Producer Eric Kripke likes to marry comedy
to horror, and slapstick to drama.

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:13.540
This expert weaving of action with witty one
liners can is clear from the shows he's produced,

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:15.040
from Supernatural to The Boys.

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:19.800
Both of these shows feature memorable tough
guys taking on supernaturally-powered people.

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:23.540
They also both feature a character named Robert
Singer, played by Jim Beaver.

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:27.700
Jim Beaver has been a part of each and every
production that Kripke has had his hand in.

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:32.200
In Supernatural, Beaver plays fan favorite
character Bobby Singer, a cantankerous old

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:36.700
hunter of demons, ghouls, and vampires, with
a fatherly influence on the show's wayward

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:37.660
main characters.

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:42.360
In The Boys, Beaver appears in the final episode
of season one as the secretary of defense,

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:46.670
also named Robert Singer, as if his appearance
alone wasn't enough of a nod to his previous

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:48.020
role in a Kripke hit show.

00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:52.920
The Boys reveals that superhero business functions
a lot like show business.

00:01:52.930 --> 00:01:55.580
Supes aren't vigilantes dedicated to justice.

00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:59.170
Instead, they're highly managed talent akin
to Hollywood actors.

00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:03.420
Vought doesn't allow them to choose where
they patrol, who they save, or even what they

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:03.920
wear.

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:08.580
All that is handled by an expert team of marketers
who look to statistics, social media, and

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:10.600
stocks in deciding what moves to make.

00:02:10.789 --> 00:02:15.060
In episode three, Starlight is given a new
costume by Vought that is a little more revealing

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:16.300
than what she's comfortable with.

00:02:16.549 --> 00:02:21.239
So Vought VP Madelyn Stillwell brings in Seth
and Evan from marketing to try to sell her

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:22.640
on the plunging neckline.

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:27.460
These names are likely purposeful nods to
the big names behind The Boys: executive producers

00:02:27.469 --> 00:02:29.170
Seth Rogen and Evan Goldberg.

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:33.569
Rogen even makes an appearance in The Boys
as himself in a promotion for the latest film

00:02:33.569 --> 00:02:34.560
in the VCU.

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:39.659
We learn in episode six that Hughie wasn't
the only person adversely affected by the

00:02:39.659 --> 00:02:44.549
superpowers of supes, as Billy Butcher brings
him to an Association of Collateral Damage

00:02:44.549 --> 00:02:45.600
Survivors meeting.

00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:50.330
In a setup that looks a lot like an Alcoholics
Anonymous meeting, survivors recount their

00:02:50.330 --> 00:02:53.390
rescues-gone-wrong and bad encounters with
supes.

00:02:53.390 --> 00:02:57.719
The wheelchair-bound woman talking as they
come in says that she wishes that a supe named

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:00.680
"Tek-Knight" would've been a little more gentle
with her spine.

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:04.740
In the comics, Tek-Knight is a kind of mashup
between Batman and Iron Man.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:07.329
He has a mansion, a butler, and a secret cave.

00:03:07.329 --> 00:03:12.719
However, he also flies around in a super-powered
metal suit and has an uncontrollable libido

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:14.780
that causes chaos for Team Payback.

00:03:14.969 --> 00:03:18.880
When it comes to this survivor of collateral
damage, it's possible that this is a dark

00:03:18.890 --> 00:03:23.489
reference to what happened with Rhodey in
the MCU, as he was shot out of the sky and

00:03:23.489 --> 00:03:28.340
crippled in Captain America: Civil War, leading
to him wearing high-tech leg braces.

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:33.400
And sure, Vision is the one who shot Rhodey
down, but ultimately, it was Iron Man's fault.

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:39.139
In episode six of The Boys, we learn that
not all superheroes are super famous.

00:03:39.139 --> 00:03:41.730
Some can be just as washed up as any old actor.

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:46.129
For example, Mother's Milk goes to the "Heroes
and Headliners" autograph show at the American

00:03:46.129 --> 00:03:50.000
Adventures Inn in order to seek out Charles,
who's better known as Mesmer.

00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:54.520
Mesmer has the power to read someone's mind
just by touching them, but his days of stardom

00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:55.500
are long gone.

00:03:55.700 --> 00:04:00.090
He was a child actor on an old show called
The Mesmerizer, in which he read the minds

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:00.980
of criminals.

00:04:01.090 --> 00:04:05.500
The Mesmerizer looks an awful lot like Law
&amp; Order, but it also riffs openly on the show

00:04:05.500 --> 00:04:10.500
that gave Neil Patrick Harris his start Doogie
Howser, M.D., in which he played a kid doctor.

00:04:10.660 --> 00:04:14.240
Mesmer's character name in The Mesmerizer
is even "Lieutenant Howser."

00:04:14.249 --> 00:04:18.769
It's notable, too, that adult Mesmer is portrayed
by Haley Joel Osment, who's best known for

00:04:18.769 --> 00:04:20.440
the roles he played as a child.

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:21.700
"I see dead people."

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:26.820
In episode three of The Boys, we learn who
the fastest man alive really is.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:27.580
Sort of.

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:32.160
Rumors had been going around that Shockwave
was actually faster than the Seven's A-Train,

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:33.600
whose only power is speed.

00:04:33.820 --> 00:04:38.099
But when the two race, A-Train ultimately
cheats by using a performance-enhancing super

00:04:38.099 --> 00:04:41.910
drug, because being slower than Shockwave
would mean that his place in the Seven would

00:04:41.910 --> 00:04:42.880
come into question.

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:46.960
After all, why would the best of the best
have the second fastest man alive?

00:04:47.140 --> 00:04:51.919
This amazing race is a reference to the many
races that the Flash and Superman have in

00:04:51.919 --> 00:04:55.169
the comics to see who was truly the speediest
speedster.

00:04:55.169 --> 00:04:59.889
Most recently, this friendly rivalry was featured
as a mid-credits scene in the Justice League

00:04:59.889 --> 00:05:03.060
movie, with the Flash and Superman getting
ready to race to the coast.

00:05:03.160 --> 00:05:05.220
Superman jokes that the stakes are high ...

00:05:06.020 --> 00:05:07.000
"But if I win, you're off the team."

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:17.610
For much of the series, we see Vought's vice
president Madelyn Stillwell making all the

00:05:17.610 --> 00:05:21.520
calls when it comes to the Seven and the other
superheroes the company manages.

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:26.320
Vought's president, known only as Mr. Edgar,
remains mysterious until the final episode

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:27.180
of season one.

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:32.220
It turns out that Mr. Edgar is played by Giancarlo
Esposito, best known for his role as the villain

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:34.120
Gustavo Fring in Breaking Bad.

00:05:34.280 --> 00:05:38.860
Mr. Edgar gives Stillwell a bonus and some
new responsibilities, obviously grooming her

00:05:38.860 --> 00:05:40.280
to eventually take his place.

00:05:40.490 --> 00:05:44.120
He says that someone will have to be around
to run the show when he moves to the tropical

00:05:44.129 --> 00:05:46.420
Central American nation of Belize.

00:05:46.420 --> 00:05:50.789
This might seem like an innocent turn of phrase,
but Breaking Bad fans know better, as Walter

00:05:50.789 --> 00:05:55.000
White and Saul Goodman use "going to Belize"
as code for having someone killed.

00:05:55.240 --> 00:05:56.340
"'Send you to Belize.'

00:05:56.350 --> 00:05:57.480
I'll send you to Belize."

00:05:57.740 --> 00:06:01.610
Maybe this is a subtle hint that by the end
of the series, the Vought president will be

00:06:01.610 --> 00:06:04.040
in the grave rather than on a sunny beach.

